# Packaging QRHype as an Android APK

QRHype is a fully client-side web app, which makes it a clean fit for
[Capacitor](https://capacitorjs.com/). Capacitor wraps the built web bundle in
a native Android shell that you can compile into an installable APK. Because the
app needs no server, the packaged APK runs completely offline.

This guide covers a local debug build. No Android build runs as part of the web
build, so these steps are done on your own machine when you want an APK.

## Prerequisites

You will need the following installed locally:

- Node.js 20 or newer and npm (already required for the web app)
- Java Development Kit 17 (Temurin or the Android Studio bundled JDK both work)
- Android Studio, which includes the Android SDK, platform tools, and an
  emulator if you want one
- The `ANDROID_HOME` environment variable pointing at your SDK location, with
  `platform-tools` on your `PATH`

Confirm the toolchain with `java -version` (should report 17) and by opening
Android Studio at least once so it finishes its first-run SDK setup.

## One-time project setup

The repository already includes `capacitor.config.ts` with these values:

- App ID: `io.dbmb.qrhype`
- App name: `QRHype`
- Web directory: `dist`

Install the Capacitor packages if they are not already present, then add the
Android platform:

```bash
npm install
npm run build
npm run android:add
```

`npm run android:add` runs `cap add android`, which creates an `android/`
directory containing the native Gradle project. This folder is generated and is
ignored by git; you can safely delete and recreate it at any time.

## Build and run cycle

Whenever you change the web app, rebuild the bundle and sync it into the native
project:

```bash
npm run build
npm run android:sync
```

`android:sync` runs `cap sync android`, copying the fresh `dist` output into the
Android project and updating native dependencies.

To open the project in Android Studio:

```bash
npm run android:open
```

From Android Studio you can run the app on an emulator or a connected device
using the Run button, and you can build signed release artifacts through
Build then Generate Signed Bundle or APK.

## Building a debug APK from the command line

If you prefer the terminal, you can assemble a debug APK directly with Gradle:

```bash
npm run android:apk
```

This runs the Gradle `assembleDebug` task inside the `android/` project. The
resulting file is written to:

```
android/app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk
```

You can install that APK on a connected device with:

```bash
adb install -r android/app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk
```

Debug APKs are signed with the shared Android debug key and are intended for
testing only.

## Producing a release APK

A release build that you can distribute needs its own signing key.

1. Generate a keystore once and keep it somewhere safe and backed up:

   ```bash
   keytool -genkey -v -keystore qrhype-release.keystore \
     -alias qrhype -keyalg RSA -keysize 2048 -validity 10000
   ```

2. In Android Studio, choose Build then Generate Signed Bundle or APK, select
   APK, and point the wizard at your keystore. Android Studio will produce a
   signed, release-ready APK.

3. Alternatively, configure the signing values in
   `android/app/build.gradle` under a `signingConfigs` block and run
   `./gradlew assembleRelease` from inside the `android/` directory.

Keep your release keystore and its passwords private. If you lose the keystore
you cannot ship updates under the same app signature.

## Updating the app icon

The Android launcher icon is generated by Capacitor from the native project. To
refresh the app branding, replace `src/assets/monogram.png` and run
`npm run icons` to regenerate the web icons, then use the Android Studio Image
Asset tool (right click the `res` folder, New then Image Asset) to regenerate
the launcher icons from your artwork.

## Troubleshooting

- If Gradle cannot find a JDK, set `JAVA_HOME` to your JDK 17 installation.
- If the SDK is missing, open Android Studio and install the SDK Platform and
  Build Tools through the SDK Manager.
- If the web content does not update in the app, rerun `npm run build` followed
  by `npm run android:sync` before rebuilding.
